I should also think about examples. Like the case of the "Bong Hits 4 Jesus" video, which was widely used to incite anti-LGBTQ+ violence. If the government had allowed it, it might have led to real-world harm. But then again, the video was just one piece of content among many; maybe the majority of content isn't as harmful. So, how do you balance preventing harm with protecting free expression?

Another angle is the difference between hate speech and other forms of speech. What's considered hate speech can be subjective. For example, a comment about a person's appearance might be seen as offensive but not necessarily hateful. There's also the question of proportionality—how much restriction is necessary to prevent harm versus the minimal intrusion into free speech.

International perspectives might vary. In some countries, hate speech is more strictly regulated because of higher rates of violence. In others, like the US, the focus is more on incitement to violence rather than just general hatred. So, context matters.

